# rules: swift-ecb-cipher
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-327-ecb.swift
# from: CWE-327 (Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm), CWE-1204, MASVS-CRYPTO-1
#
# Electronic Codebook has no chaining and no IV, so each block is enciphered on
# its own and equal plaintext blocks come out as equal ciphertext blocks. Two
# things follow, and the second is the one that gets exploited: the ciphertext
# carries the shape of the plaintext through it - the ECB penguin is the
# standard picture - and an attacker can cut blocks out of one message and
# paste them into another without ever holding the key.
#
# `kCCOptionECBMode` is opt-in. Leaving CommonCrypto's options at zero, or at
# padding alone, gives CBC with the IV that was passed, which is why the rule
# can key on the flag being written rather than on its absence.
#
# The option expression is read rather than the call, and that is what makes
# the rule work on the spelling real code uses: the flags are almost always
# OR-ed together - `kCCOptionECBMode | kCCOptionPKCS7Padding` - and a rule
# looking for the constant as a whole argument would find only the other half
# of the cases.
#
# CryptoSwift spells the same choice as a block mode object, so `ECB()` in a
# `blockMode:` argument is read too.

[ "CCOptions($O)", "CCOptions($O, $$$_)",
  "$$$C.blockMode = $O", "AES(key: $K, blockMode: $O)",
  "AES(key: $K, blockMode: $O, $$$_)" ] as $modes
| "\\bkCCOptionECBMode\\b|\\bECB\\s*\\(" as $isECB
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $modes) as $all
| ( $all | of($modes) | where_capture("O"; $isECB) | focus("O") )
| finding("swift-ecb-cipher";
    "ECB enciphers every block on its own, so equal blocks stay equal and an "
    + "attacker can rearrange them without the key; leave the options at "
    + "padding alone and pass an IV")
| report
